            <ArticleTitle>Shape-Controllable and Fluorescent Supramolecular Organic Frameworks Through Aqueous Host-Guest Complexation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>729-733</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/anie.201710553</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two kinds of shape-controllable and fluorescent supramolecular organic frameworks (cuboid or spheroid) are constructed hierarchically from CB[8] and tetraphenylethylene derivatives through host-guest interaction in water. These two fluorescent SOFs exhibit intriguing and varied photophysical properties, including large red-shifts (up to 82 nm) and stimuli-responsive behavior to competitive guest by binding with CB[8], the turn-on fluorescence of which is applied in cellular imaging.</AbstractText>
